{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011CfyjocHJMSd2KWzxqF51B","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"`--dry-run` (short form `-n`) makes rsync go through a full sync without changing anything. It works out what it would transfer, delete, or update and then stops before writing to the destination.\n\nIt's mostly used with `-v` or `--itemize-changes` (`-i`) so you can see what would happen:\n\n```bash\nrsync -avn source/ dest/\nrsync -ain --delete source/ dest/   # preview deletions, with per-file change codes\n```\n\n**What it's good for:**\n- **Checking `--delete` before it runs.** It shows which files at the destination would be removed. A missing or extra trailing slash on the source path can wipe the wrong directory.\n- **Testing `--exclude`/`--include` filters.** You can confirm the right files are matched.\n- **Checking paths and trailing slashes.** `src/` copies what's inside the folder; `src` copies the folder itself.\n\n**Limitations:**\n- Nothing actually transfers, so the speed and byte counts in the summary don't reflect a real run. The list of files is accurate, though.\n- It still connects to the remote host and reads both file trees, so it can take a while on large directories.\n- Some output can differ slightly from a real run. For example, directories that a real run would create first can't be checked further down.\n\nA common habit is to run with `-n` first, read the output, then run the same command again without `-n`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":11538,"cache_read_input_tokens":25632,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":11538},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"ff01f16c-7b3f-5ea5-8051-7a95ac7cd1d1